        cards: [
          { q: "What is a computer?", a: "An electronic device that accepts data (input), processes it according to a set of stored instructions (a program), produces information (output), and can store the data/results for future use. Its core cycle is Input → Process → Output (IPO), with Storage supporting it." },
          { q: "What was the abacus and why is it historically important?", a: "The abacus (origin in Babylon/China, c. 2400 BC) is the earliest known manual calculating/counting device. It used beads on rods to perform addition and subtraction, marking the beginning of computing aids long before electronic computers." },
          { q: "What were Napier's Bones?", a: "A manual calculating tool invented by John Napier (1617) consisting of numbered rods used to simplify multiplication and division. Napier also introduced logarithms, which aided rapid calculation." },
          { q: "Who invented the Pascaline and what did it do?", a: "Blaise Pascal invented the Pascaline (1642) - the first mechanical adding machine. It used interlocking gears/wheels to perform addition and subtraction automatically." },
          { q: "What was Leibniz's contribution to early computing?", a: "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz built the Step Reckoner (1671/1694), a mechanical calculator that could add, subtract, multiply and divide. He also formalised the binary number system used by modern computers." },
          { q: "What was the significance of the Jacquard Loom?", a: "Joseph Marie Jacquard (1801) built a weaving loom controlled by punched cards. It was the first machine to use stored instructions on punched cards, an idea later adopted in computing for input and program storage." },
          { q: "Who is regarded as the 'Father of the Computer' and why?", a: "Charles Babbage, for designing the Difference Engine and especially the Analytical Engine (1837) - a general-purpose mechanical computer whose design included an arithmetic unit, control flow, and memory, mirroring modern computer architecture." },
          { q: "Differentiate Babbage's Difference Engine from the Analytical Engine.", a: "The Difference Engine was a special-purpose machine designed only to compute polynomial/mathematical tables. The Analytical Engine was a general-purpose programmable machine with a 'mill' (processor), 'store' (memory), input and output - capable of any calculation." },
          { q: "Name the parts of the Analytical Engine and their modern equivalents.", a: "The Mill = the CPU/processor (does arithmetic); the Store = memory/RAM (holds numbers); the Input = punched cards (data/instructions); the Output = printer/punch. It conceptually matches the modern Input–Process–Output–Storage model." },
          { q: "Who was Ada Lovelace and why is she famous?", a: "Augusta Ada Lovelace is considered the first computer programmer. In her notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine she wrote an algorithm to compute Bernoulli numbers and foresaw that computers could do more than pure calculation." },
          { q: "What did Herman Hollerith contribute to computing history?", a: "Hollerith built a punched-card tabulating machine used for the 1890 US Census, drastically reducing processing time. His Tabulating Machine Company later merged to form IBM (1924)." },
          { q: "Who was Alan Turing and what is the Turing Machine?", a: "Alan Turing is regarded as the father of theoretical computer science and AI. The Turing Machine (1936) is an abstract mathematical model that defines what is computable, forming the theoretical basis of all modern computers." },
          { q: "What is the von Neumann architecture?", a: "Proposed by John von Neumann (1945), it is the stored-program concept: instructions and data are stored together in the same memory, and the CPU fetches, decodes and executes instructions sequentially. Almost all modern computers use it." },
          { q: "What was the ENIAC?", a: "Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer (1945, by Eckert & Mauchly) - one of the first general-purpose electronic digital computers. It used about 18,000 vacuum tubes, was huge, and was programmed by rewiring." },
          { q: "What was the UNIVAC?", a: "UNIVAC I (1951) was the first commercially available electronic computer in the United States. It was famously used to process census data and to predict the 1952 US presidential election." },
          { q: "What technology defined the 1st generation of computers (1940s–1956)?", a: "Vacuum tubes. They were large, generated a lot of heat, consumed enormous power, were unreliable, and used machine language. Examples: ENIAC, UNIVAC, EDVAC." },
          { q: "What technology defined the 2nd generation (1956–1963)?", a: "Transistors, which replaced vacuum tubes - smaller, faster, cheaper, more reliable, and producing less heat. Assembly language and early high-level languages (FORTRAN, COBOL) appeared." },
          { q: "What technology defined the 3rd generation (1964–1971)?", a: "Integrated Circuits (ICs) - many transistors on a single silicon chip. Computers became smaller, faster and cheaper; keyboards, monitors and operating systems came into use." },
          { q: "What technology defined the 4th generation (1971–present)?", a: "The microprocessor - an entire CPU on a single chip (e.g. the Intel 4004, 1971). It led to personal computers (PCs), GUIs, networks and the rise of high-level languages." },
          { q: "What is the focus of the 5th generation of computers?", a: "Artificial Intelligence (AI): parallel processing, natural language understanding, expert systems, robotics, and quantum/neural computing. Based on ULSI (Ultra Large Scale Integration) technology." },
          { q: "Summarise the trend across the five generations.", a: "From generation to generation: size decreases, speed increases, cost decreases, reliability increases, power consumption decreases, and storage capacity increases. Languages evolved from machine code → assembly → high-level → AI/natural language." },
          { q: "List the languages associated with each generation.", a: "1st gen: machine language. 2nd gen: assembly language. 3rd gen: high-level languages (BASIC, COBOL). 4th gen: 4GLs and application software. 5th gen: AI and natural languages (e.g. PROLOG, LISP)." },
          { q: "What is Moore's Law?", a: "Gordon Moore's observation (1965) that the number of transistors on an integrated circuit doubles roughly every 18–24 months, leading to exponential growth in computing power at decreasing cost." },
          { q: "Differentiate the mechanical era from the electronic era of computing.", a: "Mechanical era: devices used gears, wheels and punched cards (abacus, Pascaline, Analytical Engine). Electronic era: devices use electronic components (vacuum tubes, transistors, ICs, microprocessors) and operate far faster." },
          { q: "What does VLSI and ULSI stand for?", a: "VLSI = Very Large Scale Integration (thousands–millions of transistors on a chip, 4th generation). ULSI = Ultra Large Scale Integration (millions–billions of transistors, 5th generation)." },
          { q: "List the main disadvantages of first-generation computers.", a: "They were very large/bulky, extremely expensive, consumed a lot of electricity, generated excessive heat, were unreliable (tubes burned out), had limited memory and were difficult to program (machine language)." },
          { q: "What were EDVAC and EDSAC?", a: "Early stored-program computers. EDVAC (Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer, 1949) implemented von Neumann's stored-program concept; EDSAC (1949, Cambridge) was one of the first practical stored-program computers." },
          { q: "Name three key features expected of fifth-generation computers.", a: "1. Artificial intelligence and machine learning. 2. Natural language processing (understanding human speech/text). 3. Massive parallel processing and self-organising/expert systems." },
        ],
