        cards: [
          { q: "What is computer software?", a: "A set of programs, procedures and instructions that tell the hardware what to do. Unlike hardware, software is intangible (cannot be physically touched). It is broadly divided into system software and application software." },
          { q: "What is the difference between system software and application software?", a: "System software (e.g. the OS, utilities, translators) manages hardware and runs other programs. Application software performs specific user tasks (e.g. word processors, browsers, games). System software runs in the background; application software is what the user directly works with." },
          { q: "What is an Operating System (OS)?", a: "System software that manages all hardware and software resources and provides common services and an interface for programs and users (e.g. Windows, Linux, macOS, Android). It acts as the intermediary between the user and the hardware." },
          { q: "List and explain four functions of an Operating System.", a: "1. Process management - schedules and controls running programs. 2. Memory management - allocates and frees RAM. 3. File management - organises files/folders and access. 4. Device (I/O) management - controls peripherals via device drivers. (Also: security, user interface.)" },
          { q: "What is the kernel of an operating system?", a: "The kernel is the core, central component of the OS that always runs in memory. It manages the CPU, memory and devices, and provides essential low-level services to all other programs." },
          { q: "What is booting? Differentiate cold and warm booting.", a: "Booting is the process of starting up a computer and loading the OS into memory. Cold boot (cold start): turning the computer on from a powered-off state. Warm boot (restart/reset): restarting a computer that is already on, without cutting power." },
          { q: "What is the BIOS?", a: "Basic Input/Output System - firmware stored in ROM that runs first when a computer is switched on. It performs the POST (Power-On Self-Test), initialises hardware, and loads the operating system (the bootstrap process)." },
          { q: "What is firmware?", a: "Permanent software programmed into a hardware device's ROM/flash that controls the device at a low level (e.g. BIOS, firmware in printers/routers). It sits between hardware and software." },
          { q: "What is a device driver?", a: "A small program that allows the operating system to communicate with and control a specific hardware device (e.g. printer driver, graphics driver). Without the correct driver, the device may not function properly." },
          { q: "Name the main types of operating system by processing method.", a: "Batch OS, Multiprogramming OS, Time-sharing (multitasking) OS, Real-time OS (RTOS), Distributed OS, Network OS, and Multiprocessing OS." },
          { q: "What is a real-time operating system (RTOS)?", a: "An OS that processes data and responds to inputs within a strict, guaranteed time limit. Used where timing is critical - e.g. air traffic control, medical devices, industrial robots, and missile systems." },
          { q: "Differentiate single-user from multi-user operating systems.", a: "A single-user OS allows only one user at a time (e.g. MS-DOS, standard Windows desktop). A multi-user OS allows several users to access the system simultaneously, usually via terminals (e.g. UNIX, Linux servers)." },
          { q: "Differentiate multitasking from multiprocessing.", a: "Multitasking: a single CPU runs several tasks 'at once' by rapidly switching between them (time-sharing). Multiprocessing: the computer has two or more CPUs that genuinely execute multiple processes simultaneously." },
          { q: "What is the difference between a GUI and a CLI?", a: "GUI (Graphical User Interface) uses windows, icons, menus and a pointer - user-friendly and visual (e.g. Windows desktop). CLI (Command Line Interface) requires the user to type text commands - faster for experts but harder to learn (e.g. MS-DOS, terminal)." },
          { q: "What is a language translator? Name the three types.", a: "A program that converts source code written by a programmer into machine code the CPU can execute. The three types are: Assembler, Compiler, and Interpreter." },
          { q: "What is an assembler?", a: "A translator that converts assembly language (low-level mnemonic instructions) into machine language (binary). One assembly statement generally maps to one machine instruction." },
          { q: "What is the difference between a compiler and an interpreter?", a: "A compiler translates the whole source program into machine code at once before execution, producing an object/executable file (faster execution, e.g. C). An interpreter translates and executes the program line by line at run time (easier debugging, slower, e.g. Python/BASIC)." },
          { q: "What is the difference between source code and object code?", a: "Source code is the human-readable program written in a programming language. Object code is the machine-readable (binary) code produced by a compiler/assembler from the source code." },
          { q: "What is machine language?", a: "The lowest-level language consisting of binary instructions (0s and 1s) that the CPU executes directly. It is machine-dependent, fast, but very difficult for humans to write and understand." },
          { q: "What is an algorithm?", a: "A finite, ordered set of well-defined, step-by-step instructions for solving a problem or performing a task. It must terminate and produce a result." },
          { q: "What is a flowchart?", a: "A diagram that represents an algorithm or process using standard symbols (terminal, process, decision, input/output) connected by arrows showing the flow of control." },
          { q: "What is utility software? Give examples.", a: "System software that helps maintain, analyse and optimise the computer. Examples: antivirus, disk defragmenter, file compressors (WinZip/WinRAR), backup tools, and disk cleanup utilities." },
          { q: "Differentiate general-purpose from special-purpose application software.", a: "General-purpose software performs many common tasks for many users (e.g. MS Word, Excel, browsers). Special-purpose (bespoke/custom) software is written for one specific task or organisation (e.g. a bank's payroll system, hospital management software)." },
          { q: "Differentiate proprietary (closed-source) from open-source software.", a: "Proprietary software is owned by a company; its source code is hidden and it is usually paid/licensed (e.g. Windows, MS Office). Open-source software has freely available source code that anyone can view, modify and distribute (e.g. Linux, Firefox)." },
          { q: "Differentiate freeware from shareware.", a: "Freeware is fully free to use with no payment (e.g. Adobe Reader). Shareware is distributed free for a limited trial period or with limited features, after which payment is expected to continue using it fully." },
          { q: "What is the difference between a process and a program?", a: "A program is a static set of instructions stored on disk. A process is a program in execution - an active instance loaded into memory with its own resources (CPU time, memory, registers)." },
          { q: "What is spooling?", a: "Simultaneous Peripheral Operations On-Line - placing jobs/data in a buffer (e.g. on disk) so a slow device (like a printer) can process them while the CPU continues other work. Commonly used for print queues." },
          { q: "What is deadlock in an operating system?", a: "A situation where two or more processes are each waiting indefinitely for resources held by the other, so none can proceed. E.g. Process A holds resource X and waits for Y, while Process B holds Y and waits for X." },
          { q: "Name three examples of mobile operating systems.", a: "Android (Google), iOS (Apple), and HarmonyOS/KaiOS. (Older examples include Windows Phone and BlackBerry OS.)" },
          { q: "What is programming software?", a: "Software used by programmers to create, debug and maintain other software - e.g. text editors, compilers, interpreters, linkers, debuggers, and Integrated Development Environments (IDEs)." },
        ],
